List — ordered, indexed, and allows duplicates.

A list is the collection you'll reach for most. It's an ordered sequence where every element has a position — a zero-based index — order is preserved, and duplicates are allowed. If you're thinking "a bunch of things in a row", you want a list.

List is an interface, and its methods all speak in terms of position and value. You make an array-list, and add "Ada", which lands at index zero; add "Grace", at index one; and add "Ada" again — duplicates are fine — at index two. You read by index with get: get of one gives "Grace". You replace with set. Size gives you the count. Index-of gives the position of the first match. Contains tells you whether a value is present. And remove takes something away.

Now, watch that remove carefully, because there are two versions. Remove of an object removes the first matching value. Remove of an int index removes by position. They're different methods, and that's a classic trap. For a list of Integer, list dot remove of two removes the element at index two — not the value two. When you mean the value, write list dot remove of Integer dot value-of two. Keep that overload in mind.

The default implementation is array-list, and it's the right choice the vast majority of the time. Under the hood it's a resizable array. Get and set by index are constant time — instant random access. Adding at the end is amortised constant time: occasionally it grows the backing array, but on average, appending is cheap. The one weakness is inserting or removing in the middle, which is order-n, because everything after the gap has to shift down. That's fine occasionally, but avoid it in a hot loop. And if you know roughly how many elements you'll hold, new array-list with a capacity, like a thousand, pre-sizes the array and skips the regrowth.

There's also linked-list, which implements list as a doubly-linked chain of nodes. In theory, it inserts and removes in constant time, once you're already at the spot. But get by index is order-n, because it has to walk the chain, and the per-node memory overhead and poor cache behaviour make it slower than array-list in practice. So here's the honest guidance: default to array-list, and reach for linked-list almost never. And when you want a queue or a stack, use array-deque, which we'll meet shortly — not linked-list.

Finally, a list keeps insertion order, so iterating gives elements back in the sequence you added them. Java twenty-one formalises this "has a first and last, in order" idea with the sequenced-collection interface, which list now implements. That gives you handy endpoint methods: get-first, get-last, add-first, add-last, and reversed. So get-first is clearer than get of zero, and get-last is clearer than get of size minus one. Reach for a list whenever order matters, or duplicates are meaningful — a log of events, items in a cart, the rows of a result. When you instead care about "is this present?", with no duplicates, that's a set — which is next.
